// Generated file. Regenerate with scripts/generate-event-pages-en.mjs.
window.EVENT_EN_DATA = {"kernel-power|41":{"slug":"kernel-power-41","t":"Kernel-Power Event 41: Causes and What to Check","s":"Kernel-Power 41 is a result record: Windows booted after the previous session ended without a normal shutdown. It does not say why.","c":["Back up open files, then note the time of the event and what the PC was doing.","On the Details tab of event 41, check whether BugcheckCode is 0, then look at WHEA-Logger and Display events in the 5 minutes before it.","Compare CPU/GPU temperatures and power-limit flags from a tool such as HWiNFO."],"w":["Do not buy a PSU or edit registry values based on event 41 alone. Whether it also happens at idle, only in one game, or together with WHEA 18 or Display 4101 is a much better clue.","Burning smell, discoloured cables, repeated instant power-offs or power loss while saving: reduce use, back up, and check the power path first."]},"eventlog|6008":{"slug":"eventlog-6008","t":"EventLog Event 6008: Unexpected Shutdown Explained","s":"6008 is an after-the-fact report that the previous system shutdown ended unexpectedly. It does not contain the cause.","c":["Write down the previous shutdown time given in the description; every later check is relative to it.","In Event Viewer, narrow to a few minutes around that time and look at Error/Critical records: BugCheck 1001, WHEA-Logger and Disk events point to the cause.","Open Reliability Monitor (Control Panel → View reliability history) for a day-by-day overview of failures."],"w":["It is usually the record of a shutdown result, not of the cause."]},"user32|1074":{"slug":"user32-1074","t":"User32 Event 1074: Planned Shutdown or Restart","s":"1074 is an information record written when Windows receives a shutdown or restart request. Unlike Kernel-Power 41 it does not mean power was cut abruptly.","c":["Record the process, user and Reason Code from the description.","Compare with Windows Update and installer events at the same time.","Check whether an actual reboot followed 1074 by comparing times with 6008 and Kernel-Power 41."],"w":["1074 is a record of a planned shutdown request. Do not conclude from it alone that a shutdown was unwanted or malicious."]},"applicationerror|1000":{"slug":"application-error-1000","t":"Application Error Event 1000: When an App Keeps Crashing","s":"When a program ends because of an exception it did not handle, event 1000 can appear in the Application log.","c":["Record the app version, faulting module, exception code and what you were doing just before the crash.","Compare whether only one app repeats or several apps crash with different modules.","Repair or update the app, then retest with plug-ins and overlays turned off."],"w":["If several apps crash with different faulting modules and you also see blue screens or file corruption, treat it as a system problem: check RAM, storage and stability first."]},"applicationhang|1002":{"slug":"application-hang-1002","t":"Application Hang Event 1002: Program Not Responding","s":"1002 is logged when a program stops responding for a period of time and Windows marks it as hung. Unlike Application Error 1000 the process was not ended by an exception.","c":["Record the name of the hung app and the hang type.","Compare whether it reproduces only in the same app.","Turn off overlays and plug-ins, and retest from a clean boot."],"w":["Do not conclude RAM or storage failure from 1002 alone. The app that hung and the conditions are better clues."]},"windowserrorreporting|1001":{"slug":"wer-1001","t":"Windows Error Reporting Event 1001: What It Records","s":"1001 records that a blue screen, an app crash or a hardware error was filed as a report in Windows Error Reporting. The event itself does not mean a blue screen.","c":["Identify the report type first: BlueScreen/BugcheckCode means a blue screen, an executable name means an app crash.","For a blue screen, convert BugcheckCode to hexadecimal and look up the stop code. If a dump exists in C:\\Windows\\Minidump, it can narrow the cause to a driver.","For an app crash, also look at Application Error 1000, which records the faulting module and exception code."],"w":["Do not assume a blue screen before you have checked the report type."]},"bugcheck|1001":{"slug":"bugcheck-1001","t":"BugCheck Event 1001: Blue Screen Details in the Log","s":"BugCheck 1001 is the record Windows leaves in the System log right after a blue screen (stop error). The stop code, four parameters and the dump file path are recorded together, so it is the first source for the analysis.","c":["Read the stop code (for example 0x0000009c) and the four parameters in the General tab.","Compare WHEA-Logger and Kernel-Power events at the same time.","Analyze the dump files in C:\\Windows\\Minidump with a dump analyzer."],"w":["The record itself is not the cause; it only states that a crash happened and its details. Use the stop code to narrow the candidates."]},"disk|7":{"slug":"disk-7","t":"Disk Event 7: Bad Block on a Drive — Back Up First","s":"Disk 7 records that an unreadable block was detected on a storage device. If it repeats, consider the risk of data loss first.","c":["Back up important files to a different physical drive or the cloud first.","Identify the disk number and model; check SMART with CrystalDiskInfo or the vendor tool.","Swap the data cable and port (SATA) or re-seat the module (NVMe) and compare."],"w":["Do not run chkdsk repair options or write-heavy scans repeatedly before backing up: cleaning up the file system can add load to a physically unstable device. Normal SMART does not prove the device is healthy if the events and real read failures keep repeating.","Replacement becomes a priority if reallocated/pending sectors grow, the maker's diagnostic fails, the same error appears with another cable or port, or unreadable files appear."]},"disk|51":{"slug":"disk-51","t":"Disk Event 51: An Error During a Paging Operation","s":"51 is a warning that storage I/O was delayed or failed during a paging operation. It is classed below the error level of event 7, but if its frequency grows it can be an early sign of the same storage problem.","c":["Identify the disk: read N in \\Device\\HarddiskN\\DR in the description and compare it with the disk number in diskmgmt.msc.","Back up important files first — storage response is abnormal, so backup comes before scanning.","Check SMART in CrystalDiskInfo: reallocated, pending and uncorrectable sectors. If they are non-zero and rising, consider replacement."],"w":["The more it repeats, the higher the risk of data loss."]},"ntfs|55":{"slug":"ntfs-55","t":"Ntfs Event 55: File System Corruption — Back Up Before chkdsk","s":"55 records that corruption was detected in the NTFS file-system structure. It can come from an abnormal shutdown or from the storage device itself. Back up the data before running any repair command.","c":["Back up important data first. Some files may be moved to found.000 or lost during repair.","Check drive health: look at SMART in CrystalDiskInfo. If the status is “Caution” or reallocated sectors are rising, consider replacement before scanning.","Run the scan when there is time: chkdsk C: /f from an administrator prompt schedules a check on reboot. Large drives can take hours, so start when you do not need to cut power."],"w":["If there are signs of drive failure, do not start a write-heavy repair first."]},"distributedcom|10016":{"slug":"distributedcom-10016","t":"DistributedCOM Event 10016: Permission Warning — Usually Safe to Ignore","s":"10016 is a warning that a specific COM component did not get the local launch/activation permission it requested. It piles up on perfectly healthy PCs and is usually not urgent.","c":["First check whether anything actually failed at the same time. If there is no visible symptom, there is nothing to fix.","Note the CLSID/APPID from the description and see whether the same value repeats. If it differs every time, it is hard to call it a problem of one component.","Do not change permissions at random. Editing permissions in Component Services is widely described, but changing the default design can weaken security and is not recommended before a real failure is confirmed."],"w":["Do not follow registry or DCOM permission changes from the internet as they are."]},"servicecontrolmanager|7000":{"slug":"service-7000","t":"Service Control Manager Event 7000: Service Failed to Start","s":"7000 records that a Windows service failed to start. It is safer to identify which program the service belongs to first than to delete it blindly.","c":["Identify the service name and the error text. “The system cannot find the file specified” means the executable is gone; “Access is denied” is a log-on account permission problem; “did not respond in a timely fashion” should be read together with 7009 as a delay problem.","Check that the executable still exists: run sc qc <service name> from an administrator prompt and paste BINARY_PATH_NAME into File Explorer. A service registration left behind after the program was removed is the most common cause.","Check the log-on account and password: in services.msc open the service, Log On tab. If a service that was Local System now uses a specific user account whose password recently changed, it will fail to start; re-enter or reassign the account."],"w":["Do not disable services at random."]},"servicecontrolmanager|7034":{"slug":"service-7034","t":"Service Control Manager Event 7034: Service Terminated Unexpectedly","s":"7034 is logged when a service terminated unexpectedly and no recovery action was recorded. Check whether you actually use that service before judging how relevant it is.","c":["Identify the service and the program it belongs to: open it in services.msc to read the description and executable path.","Use the Recovery tab to separate it from 7031: if the recovery action is “Take No Action”, Windows has nothing to schedule, so 7034 is logged instead of 7031.","Compare when the records began with program installs or Windows updates around that date; if it started at a specific time, reverting that change is the fastest check."],"w":["First check whether the service is one you do not use."]},"display|4101":{"slug":"display-4101","t":"Display Event 4101: Graphics Driver Stopped Responding and Recovered","s":"Event 4101 is mainly recorded when the GPU fails to respond within the allowed time and Windows restarts the graphics driver (Timeout Detection and Recovery).","c":["Record which app, the time, and whether the screen came back or the PC rebooted.","Check the driver version and faulting module in dxdiag and the event.","Restore any GPU overclock and undervolt to defaults."],"w":["Stop load tests immediately if you see screen corruption, a stopped fan or a burning smell. Run short comparisons only when there are no signs of hardware damage."]},"whea-logger|1":{"slug":"whea-logger-1","t":"WHEA-Logger Event 1: Uncorrectable Hardware Error Record","s":"WHEA-Logger 1 records an uncorrectable hardware error as an error record. Unlike 18, you must read the shown error source and type yourself to tell whether the CPU, PCIe or memory layer is involved.","c":["Record ErrorSource, ErrorType, Processor APIC ID and PCIe device information from Details.","Turn off XMP/EXPO and overclocks and compare whether it reproduces at default settings.","Check BIOS, chipset, GPU and SSD firmware against the vendors' information."],"w":["Do not decide on a specific part from WHEA 1 alone. The source of the error record and the conditions under which it repeats matter more.","Do not repeat long stress tests without a backup."]},"whea-logger|18":{"slug":"whea-logger-18","t":"WHEA-Logger Event 18: Fatal Hardware Error — What to Check","s":"WHEA 18 is Windows reporting a hardware error it could not correct. If it repeats, a backup and a return to default settings come first.","c":["Back up important files to another drive.","Reset overclock, undervolt, PBO and XMP/EXPO to defaults.","Record Error Type, APIC ID and time; see whether it repeats in the same task."],"w":["Do not turn off the WHEA service or raise voltage at random to hide the error. Repeating long stress tests without knowing the cause increases the risk of data damage and overheating. If it repeats even at default settings, go on to the maker's diagnostics and part cross-testing.","Kernel-Power 41 is the reboot result and BugCheck 0x124 is the hardware-error stop code. If all three have the same time, compare them as one failure."]},"security|4625":{"slug":"security-4625","t":"Security Event 4625: An Account Failed to Log On","s":"4625 records that an account failed to log on. The failure reason code and the logon type (interactive / remote / network) tell a simple typo apart from an external access attempt.","c":["Check the failure reason code (such as 0xC000006D) and the logon type.","See whether only a specific account fails repeatedly.","For remote access, check the source IP."],"w":["Repeated failures on the same account clustered in a short time may be a security threat."]},"security|4740":{"slug":"security-4740","t":"Security Event 4740: A User Account Was Locked Out","s":"4740 records that an account was locked out. In most cases it is the result of 4625 (failed logon) repeating over a short time. The caller computer name identifies which device kept failing.","c":["Use the caller computer name in the event to find the device causing the lockout.","Delete or update the account's entries in Credential Manager.","Update the password of the accounts used by scheduled tasks and services."],"w":["Repeated lockouts are often caused by expired credentials stored in several places."]},"security|1102":{"slug":"security-1102","t":"Security Event 1102: The Audit Log Was Cleared","s":"1102 records that the security audit log was cleared. An administrator may have tidied the log on purpose, but it can also appear when someone tries to erase traces after a compromise, so you must find who cleared it and when.","c":["Check the account name and time of the person who cleared the log.","Compare unusual sign-ins or program executions before and after the clearing.","If the clearing was not intended, carry out a security check."],"w":["An unintended clearing of the security log can be a sign of a security incident."]},"windowsupdateclient|20":{"slug":"windowsupdateclient-20","t":"WindowsUpdateClient Event 20: Update Installation Failed","s":"Event 20 records that the Windows Update Agent could not complete the installation of a specific update. Do not judge that all of Windows is broken from this event alone; first check the failed KB and the accompanying error code.","c":["Identify the failed KB: in Settings → Windows Update → Update history, see whether the same KB fails repeatedly.","Record the error code, such as 0x800F0922, 0x80070002 or 0x80070005. If none is shown, read the Data value in the event XML.","Check the basics: free space on the system drive, automatic date and time, VPN/proxy and the network connection."],"w":["Event 20 tells you which update failed. It is not a diagnosis that confirms the cause on its own. Record the KB number, errorCode and the repeat conditions together so the next check is accurate."]},".netruntime|1026":{"slug":"dotnet-runtime-1026","t":".NET Runtime Event 1026: Unhandled .NET Exception","s":".NET Runtime 1026 records that a program running on .NET hit an unhandled exception and the process ended. Check the target app and the exception type before reinstalling anything.","c":["Record the exception text and the app name.","Compare the faulting module and exception code in Application Error 1000.","Repair the app, then check the required .NET components and updates."],"w":["Do not uninstall and reinstall .NET on the strength of 1026 alone. Check the exception type and the version the app requires first."]},"servicecontrolmanager|7001":{"slug":"service-7001","t":"Service Control Manager Event 7001: Dependency Service Failed","s":"7001 records that a service could not start because another service it depends on did not start. Check the name of the dependency shown in the description first.","c":["Identify the dependency in the description. The wording is “The X service depends on the Y service, which failed to start”. The service to fix is Y, not the X that is named prominently; restarting only X makes the same record repeat.","Find the 7000/7023 record for Y from the same time: sort Event Viewer by time and look a few seconds before 7001. The real error text for Y is there and is the starting point.","Follow the dependency tree to the end: in services.msc open Y's properties and the Dependencies tab. If Y depends on yet another service, check the very start of the chain; fixing a middle service does not help if the first one still fails."],"w":["Do not edit the dependency registry entries at random."]},"servicecontrolmanager|7009":{"slug":"service-7009","t":"Service Control Manager Event 7009: Service Connection Timeout","s":"7009 records that a service did not connect within the time limit. Finding what causes the delay, such as a disk or network bottleneck, matters more than the service itself.","c":["Check the service name and the time waited: the number in brackets is the milliseconds Windows waited. The default is 30000 (30 seconds). The point of the record is which service did not respond within it.","Look at disk and network events from the same time: if Disk 51/153 or storahci 129 appear at the same moment, the cause is storage delay, not the service, so check the disk first.","Separate “only right after boot”: if so, start-up resource contention is likely. Reduce high-impact items on Task Manager's Startup tab and see whether disk usage stays at 100% for long after boot."],"w":["Find the cause of the delay before lengthening the time limit."]},"servicecontrolmanager|7011":{"slug":"service-7011","t":"Service Control Manager Event 7011: Service Control Request Timeout","s":"7011 records that a service did not respond to a control request in time. It often appears with slow shutdown or reboot.","c":["Record the service name and when it happened: this event is also written after the default 30000 ms wait. Note whether it happened during shutdown or boot, which narrows the next step a lot.","Check whether it comes with slow shutdown: if the “Shutting down services” screen lingers, the service is probably not answering control requests, that is, deadlocked. If EventLog 6008 is also present, it led to a forced shutdown.","Check for 129/153 alongside: if storahci 129 or Disk 153 are present at the same time, storage delay blocked the service response. Check cables, NVMe slot and firmware first."],"w":["Force-deleting a service is the last resort."]},"servicecontrolmanager|7023":{"slug":"service-7023","t":"Service Control Manager Event 7023: Service Terminated With an Error","s":"7023 records that a service ended after returning its own error code. The wording at the end of the description, read together with the service name, narrows the cause.","c":["Read the error wording in the description: because 7023 means the service ended with its own error, the text at the end is the clue. Write it down exactly, for example “Access is denied” or “The specified service is not installed”.","Look up the wording together with the service's purpose: the same wording needs different action per service, so search the service name and the wording together and see which feature it belongs to.","Check dependencies and the log-on account: look at the Dependencies and Log On tabs in services.msc. A permission-type message points to the log-on account; a start-failure message points to a prerequisite service."],"w":["Restarting the service without the error text can hide the cause of recurrence."]},"servicecontrolmanager|7031":{"slug":"service-7031","t":"Service Control Manager Event 7031: Service Terminated Unexpectedly","s":"7031 records that a service ended unexpectedly and Windows scheduled a recovery action such as a restart. Whether the count keeps growing is what decides if action is needed.","c":["Check the service name and the count: in “This action was performed N time(s)” N shows how much it repeats. If N keeps growing it is a reproducible problem, not a transient error.","Check whether Application Error 1000 is present: 7031 reports only the result, not the faulting module. The 1000 from the same time holds the module name and exception code, and the real investigation should start from that module.","Check the recovery settings: in services.msc → the service's properties → Recovery tab, look at the first and second failure actions. Restart is configured there, which is why 7031 is logged; the setting itself is not the problem."],"w":["Act when the repeat count increases."]},"storahci|129":{"slug":"storahci-129","t":"storahci Event 129: Storage Device Reset — What to Check","s":"Event 129 is recorded when a storage request does not complete in time and Windows resets the controller. Split the causes into connection, firmware and power management.","c":["Back up important data and check whether Disk 7, 51 or 153 appear at the same time.","Check firmware, health, temperature and error history with the SSD maker's tool.","Cross-check the cable and port for SATA, or the slot and cooling for NVMe."],"w":["Do not change power-management or timeout registry values just to make the event go away."]},"disk|153":{"slug":"disk-153","t":"Disk Event 153: Repeated I/O Retries on a Drive","s":"153 records that a disk I/O operation was retried. It can be a temporary delay or an early clue to a connection problem; check whether it grows on the same disk together with events 7 and 129.","c":["Check the disk and the frequency: match the disk number in the description with diskmgmt.msc to identify the device, and filter Event Viewer by this ID to count how many a day. A change in frequency is the indicator of getting worse.","Check whether 129 or 7 come with it: if storahci 129 (device reset) or Disk 7 (bad block) appear at the same time, it is past a temporary delay. Raise the backup priority.","Check SMART and the connection: check status in CrystalDiskInfo; for SATA recheck cable and port, for NVMe recheck the slot seating."],"w":["If it repeats, do not dismiss it as a simple performance issue."]},"ntfs|98":{"slug":"ntfs-98","t":"Ntfs Event 98: Volume Is Healthy — A Normal Record","s":"Ntfs 98 appears as “Volume … is healthy. No action is needed.” It means NTFS checked the volume and found no problem with the file system.","c":["Check the CorruptionAction value (if healthy, no action).","Check whether the same volume has errors other than Ntfs 55/98.","When storage errors are suspected, use a volume with this record as a hint that file-system errors are unlikely."],"w":["It is a normal record. It does not guarantee the physical disk is healthy, so check SMART too."]},"ntfs|140":{"slug":"ntfs-140","t":"Ntfs Event 140: Transaction Log Flush Failed","s":"Ntfs 140 records that NTFS could not write data to the transaction log and warns the volume may be corrupted. Look at the storage I/O path and file-system state together.","c":["Back up important files first and note the event's VolumeId and DeviceName.","Compare whether Disk 7, 11, 51, 98, 129 or 153 appear at the same time.","Check SMART, cable, port and M.2 seating."],"w":["140 alone does not mean files are already damaged, but if it repeats, reduce writes and prioritize backup because of the risk of data loss."]},"volsnap|36":{"slug":"volsnap-36","t":"volsnap Event 36: Shadow Copy Storage Limit Reached","s":"volsnap 36 records that a volume shadow copy was aborted because of the configured shadow copy storage limit. System Restore, Windows Backup or backup software may not be able to create new copies.","c":["Note the source volume and the number of the stopped shadow copy shown in the event.","Check the dedicated storage and existing copies with vssadmin list shadowstorage and vssadmin list shadows.","In System Protection, check the maximum usage for that volume and whether you really need restore points."],"w":["The shadow-copy-specific limit can be too small even when the disk has plenty of free space.","Running vssadmin delete shadows /all carelessly can delete existing restore points and backup copies, so check the target and your recovery plan first."]},"eventlog|6005":{"slug":"eventlog-6005","t":"EventLog Event 6005: Windows Start Time","s":"6005 is an information record that the Windows Event Log service started. It is normally used as a reference point for the boot time and does not mean an error or an abnormal shutdown.","c":["Compare the 6005 time with Kernel-Power 41, EventLog 6008 and User32 1074.","Check whether the boot time differs from the time you actually powered on.","If it repeats in a short time, check why the Event Log service restarted and the 7000-series events."],"w":["6005 is in most cases a normal boot or service-start record. Do not judge the cause of a reboot from this event alone."]},"eventlog|6006":{"slug":"eventlog-6006","t":"EventLog Event 6006: Was the Shutdown Clean?","s":"6006 is an information record that the Event Log service stopped as part of the shutdown procedure. If 6006 is present and 6008 is absent, the pattern is close to a normal shutdown.","c":["Compare the 6006 time with the shutdown request time in User32 1074.","Check whether 6005 was recorded again in the same boot cycle.","If only 6008 and Kernel-Power 41 exist without 6006, first check for a power cut or forced shutdown."],"w":["6006 itself is not an error. Separate whether the absence of a normal-shutdown record is the problem or whether you actually forced a shutdown."]},"tpm-wmi|1801":{"slug":"tpm-wmi-1801","t":"TPM-WMI Event 1801: Secure Boot Certificate Update Needed","s":"1801 records that an updated Secure Boot certificate is available but has not yet been applied to the firmware. It relates to Microsoft's ongoing rollout of renewed Secure Boot certificates, as the 2011 certificates approach expiry.","c":["In Settings → Windows Update, check for pending updates or a pending reboot.","In the update history, check whether Secure Boot–related updates were applied.","On the motherboard or laptop maker's site, check for and apply the latest BIOS/UEFI firmware."],"w":["Do not turn Secure Boot off on your own. The certificate renewal is routine security maintenance that proceeds automatically. A power loss during a firmware update can damage the motherboard, so keep a laptop plugged in."]},"nvlddmkm|153":{"slug":"nvlddmkm-153","t":"nvlddmkm Event 153: NVIDIA Graphics Driver Recovery","s":"nvlddmkm 153 is a system event the NVIDIA graphics kernel driver can leave while recovering the GPU's response. Its meaning differs from Disk 153, so check that the source is nvlddmkm.","c":["Record the Device\\Video number and TDR wording in the event XML, the app and the driver version.","If it began right after a driver update, remove the NVIDIA driver in Safe Mode and install a stable version fresh.","Check GPU temperature, hot spot, power, and the 8-pin/12V connection, and restore overclock/undervolt to defaults."],"w":["Do not conclude a failed graphics card or power supply from nvlddmkm 153 alone.","If you see screen corruption, a burning smell or discoloured connectors, stop load tests, unplug the power and inspect."]},"servicecontrolmanager|7045":{"slug":"service-7045","t":"Service Control Manager Event 7045: A New Service Was Installed","s":"7045 is an audit record that a new service or a kernel-driver service was installed. It is not an error; it records what was installed and when.","c":["Record the service name, service file path, account and start type.","Check that the time matches a program or driver you installed.","Check the file signature and publisher."],"w":["7045 is an install audit record, not an error. Even a Microsoft or vendor path can have an unfamiliar name, so check the file signature and path together.","Before deleting or disabling a service right away, confirm how to undo it and who installed it."]},"schannel|36874":{"slug":"schannel-36874","t":"Schannel Event 36874: TLS Negotiation Failed","s":"36874 records that a TLS connection failed because the client and server could not find a common encryption method or protocol. First read the description to see which app failed talking to which server.","c":["Check the Protocol, the error state and the calling process in the event.","Compare with 36888 and any app or Windows Update failures from the same time.","Check the system date and time, and the root certificate updates."],"w":["Do not enable TLS 1.0/1.1 unconditionally to lower security, and do not just hide the Schannel log.","If you run the server, check both the client and server settings together."]},"windowsupdateclient|31":{"slug":"windowsupdateclient-31","t":"WindowsUpdateClient Event 31: Update Download Failed","s":"31 records that Windows Update failed to download an update file. Unlike a failure at the install step (event 20), the download stage was blocked, so look at the network, services and storage space first.","c":["Record the update name and the error code in the event body.","Check the internet connection and whether a VPN or proxy is in use.","Check that the Windows Update and BITS services are running."],"w":["Do not delete the SoftwareDistribution folder by hand during an update. If needed, stop the services and back up important files first.","If the same update fails repeatedly, first record the update number (KB) and the error code."]},"windowsdefender|1116":{"slug":"defender-1116","t":"Windows Defender Event 1116: Malware Detected — Check 1117 for the Action","s":"Event 1116 records that Microsoft Defender Antivirus detected malware or a potentially unwanted application (PUA). It records only the detection; whether the threat was quarantined or removed is shown in the Action value of the following event 1117.","c":["Record the threat name, severity and detected file path from the event description.","Find the 1117 event from the same time or just after it and check whether the Action is quarantine, remove, clean or allow.","In Windows Security → Virus & threat protection → Protection history, check that the same item is listed."],"w":["Do not register a detected file as an exception at once just because a game or work needs it. Search the threat name on Microsoft Security Intelligence and check its real nature before deciding. Conversely, you do not need to reinstall the whole system because 1116 appeared once; check the result in 1117 first."]},"kernel-power|109":{"slug":"kernel-power-109","t":"Kernel-Power Event 109: Shutdown Transition Started","s":"Kernel-Power 109 records that the kernel power manager started a shutdown (or restart) transition. It is an information record also left by a normal shutdown from the Start menu, the power button or a program's request.","c":["Check the Action (shutdown method) and the Reason value.","Check whether Kernel-Power 41 (abnormal shutdown) followed at the same time.","Compare the time with User32 1074 (who requested the shutdown)."],"w":["It is an informational record and not a problem alone. If neither 41 nor 6008 follows it, a normal shutdown is the right reading."]}};
